{
  "analysis": "The user reports they can't curl example.com. The patch history shows a prior benchmark task that involved installing curl via apt. I need to diagnose why curl fails - could be curl not installed, DNS issues, network/proxy issues, or firewall. Let me start investigating.",
  "plan": "First check if curl is installed and what happens when we try to curl. Then check DNS resolution, network connectivity, and any environment/proxy configuration. I'll run several diagnostic commands to narrow down the cause.",
  "commands": [
    {
      "keystrokes": "which curl; echo '---'; curl -v example.com 2>&1 | head -n 30\n",
      "duration": 3.0
    },
    {
      "keystrokes": "echo \"http_proxy=$http_proxy https_proxy=$https_proxy no_proxy=$no_proxy\"\n",
      "duration": 0.1
    }
  ]
}

{
  "analysis": "curl is installed at /usr/bin/curl, no proxy is set, but curl fails with 'DNS resolution error'. This is a DNS resolution problem, not a connectivity or proxy issue. I need to investigate DNS configuration: /etc/resolv.conf, the resolver, and whether name resolution works at all.",
  "plan": "Check /etc/resolv.conf for nameservers, check if there's any resolver config, try resolving example.com with dig/host/getent, and test basic connectivity. This will tell me whether nameservers are misconfigured, missing, or if DNS traffic is blocked.",
  "commands": [
    {
      "keystrokes": "cat /etc/resolv.conf 2>&1; echo '--- nsswitch ---'; grep hosts /etc/nsswitch.conf 2>&1\n",
      "duration": 0.1
    },
    {
      "keystrokes": "getent hosts example.com; echo \"getent_rc=$?\"; echo '---'; nslookup example.com 2>&1 | head -n 10; echo '---'; host example.com 2>&1 | head\n",
      "duration": 3.0
    }
  ]
}

{
  "analysis": "Key finding: the system resolver works (getent hosts example.com returns IPv6 addresses, rc=0). The nameserver is 127.0.0.11 (Docker's embedded DNS). But curl specifically fails with 'DNS resolution error' while getent succeeds. This points to a curl-specific issue rather than a system DNS failure. Likely causes: a curl config file (~/.curlrc or /etc/curlrc) passing bad options, curl using a different resolver library, or an IPv4/IPv6 (Happy Eyeballs) interaction. I need to dig into curl's behavior.",
  "plan": "Check for curl config files that might inject bad options. Run curl with -4 and -6 to see if one family works. Capture the full verbose output. Also test curl against the embedded resolver with a public DNS to isolate whether it's curl's internal resolver that's broken.",
  "commands": [
    {
      "keystrokes": "ls -la /etc/curlrc ~/.curlrc 2>&1; echo '---'; cat /etc/curlrc 2>/dev/null; cat ~/.curlrc 2>/dev/null\n",
      "duration": 0.1
    },
    {
      "keystrokes": "curl -4 -v example.com 2>&1 | head -n 15; echo '=== IPV6 ==='; curl -6 -v example.com 2>&1 | head -n 15\n",
      "duration": 5.0
    }
  ]
}

{
  "analysis": "No curlrc config files exist. Both -4 and -6 fail with 'DNS resolution error', yet getent resolves fine, and there's no proxy. So curl's internal resolver fails while the system resolver (via nsswitch: files dns) works. I need to understand why curl's resolver specifically fails. Let me check what curl actually is (binary vs wrapper), its version, and whether the failure is consistent across domains.",
  "plan": "Check curl's type/version, whether it might be a shell wrapper/alias, and test resolution against a couple of different hostnames. Also try curl with --dns-servers to point directly at a public resolver, which would tell us if it's a nameserver selection issue. This isolates whether curl is misconfigured to use a resolver that fails.",
  "commands": [
    {
      "keystrokes": "type curl; file $(which curl); curl --version 2>&1 | head -n 3\n",
      "duration": 0.1
    },
    {
      "keystrokes": "curl -v http://google.com 2>&1 | head -n 5; echo '==='; curl -v http://1.1.1.1 2>&1 | head -n 8\n",
      "duration": 5.0
    }
  ]
}

{
  "analysis": "Critical clue: `curl --version` and even `curl http://1.1.1.1` (a literal IP requiring NO DNS) both return 'DNS resolution error'. That is impossible for a real curl binary — a literal IP never triggers DNS. This strongly suggests /usr/bin/curl is not a real binary but a wrapper/mock script that always prints 'DNS resolution error' regardless of arguments. I need to inspect the actual file content.",
  "plan": "View the contents of /usr/bin/curl to confirm whether it's a script masquerading as curl. If it's a fake wrapper, the fix is to replace it with the real curl binary (e.g., from the system package or a proper curl). I'll cat the file and check its first bytes.",
  "commands": [
    {
      "keystrokes": "wc -c /usr/bin/curl; echo '--- head ---'; head -n 40 /usr/bin/curl\n",
      "duration": 0.1
    },
    {
      "keystrokes": "head -c 4 /usr/bin/curl | od -c\n",
      "duration": 0.1
    }
  ]
}

{
  "analysis": "Root cause found. /usr/bin/curl is NOT a real curl binary — it's a 46-byte shell script that always prints 'DNS resolution error' and exits 11, no matter what arguments you pass (it even fails on the literal IP http://1.1.1.1, which would never need DNS). So the entire 'DNS' investigation was a red herring; the real curl binary has been replaced/clobbered by a fake wrapper. The system's DNS is actually fine (getent resolves example.com correctly). The fix is to restore the genuine curl binary by reinstalling the curl package.",
  "plan": "Reinstall the curl package via apt so the real binary overwrites the fake script. First verify apt is functional, then run apt-get install --reinstall curl (falling back to a fresh install). After that, confirm curl --version works and curl actually fetches example.com.",
  "commands": [
    {
      "keystrokes": "apt-get install --reinstall -y curl 2>&1 | tail -n 15\n",
      "duration": 15.0
    }
  ]
}

